WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
Let u be odd and greater than one.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:06.140
Our all-ones polynomial J of
P is the sum of P to the r,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.080
from r equal to zero
through u squared minus one.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.140
Let I contain the distinct
primes dividing u.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.620
Choose a primitive p-th
root for each prime p.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.460
For a subset S, multiply its
chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.140
The empty subset gives the input
one; set it aside for the next node.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.760
Our target is exact zero at
every nonempty-subset input.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.340
For a nonempty subset, put m equal to
the product of its distinct primes.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.380
The root zeta sub S raised to m
equals one, so its order divides m.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.640
Fix a prime p in S.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.140
Raising to m over p removes
all the other root factors.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.100
The remaining factor is zeta
sub p raised to m over p.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.140
This is not one, because p
does not divide m over p.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.060
The order therefore contains every
prime in the squarefree number m,

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:02.640
and must equal m.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.880
For example, take u equal to fifteen
and S containing three and five.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.450
The fifth power of the product
of zeta three and zeta five

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.340
leaves zeta three to the
fifth, which is nontrivial.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.050
The third power leaves zeta
five cubed, also nontrivial.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.180
The product has order fifteen. In general,

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:26.580
nonempty S gives m greater than
one, and m divides u squared.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.870
Thus zeta sub S is not one,
while its u-squared power is one.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.830
Now use a formal polynomial identity.
Multiply J of P by P minus one.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.100
The shifted row P times J has
powers one through u squared.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.900
Subtracting J cancels every
matching power from P through

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.020
P to the exponent u squared minus one.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.420
Only P raised to u squared,
minus one, remains.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.820
Therefore the product of P minus
one and J of P equals the difference

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.840
between P raised to u squared
and one. Substitute the root.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.420
Its u-squared power is one,
so zeta sub S minus one,

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.915
multiplied by J of zeta
sub S, equals zero.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:17.640
These evaluated values lie in the
characteristic-zero domain A inside

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.560
the complex numbers. The
first factor is nonzero.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.170
Cancel it in that domain to obtain
J of zeta sub S equal to zero.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:30.020
This is exact, before any
reduction modulo one plus i.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:36.060
For u equal to three, let omega be the
exponential of two pi i over three.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.200
The nine powers repeat one, omega,
and omega squared three times.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:45.640
J of omega equals three times the quantity
one plus omega plus omega squared,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.000
which is zero. Draw the three unit
vectors in each group head to tail.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.060
Each triple closes into an equilateral
triangle, so its sum is zero.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.700
All three groups vanish exactly.
The geometric-sum argument

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.680
proves the same cancellation
for every nonempty subset.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.700
The waiting input P equal to one
is excluded from that conclusion.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:08.180
It behaves differently, and
the next node evaluates it.
